<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tepehuán Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1616">1616</a>–<a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tepehuán Revolt was a rising of the Tepehuán people of Nueva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Vizcaya</a>, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, against Spanish rule that broke out in November 1616. Led by a prophet who promised to drive out the Spaniards, the rebels killed settlers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> and Franciscan missionaries and destroyed missions and mining settlements around Durango. Spanish forces had suppressed it by about 1620, leaving the region badly depopulated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tepehuan-revolt.jpg" alt="Tepehuán Revolt" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Smallchief · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tepuhuan_location_1616.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Tepehuán lived on the eastern slopes and valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-madre-occidental">Sierra Madre Occidental</a>, mostly in what became the state of Durango. They spoke a Uto-Aztecan language and lived chiefly by farming, unlike the nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimeca</a> to the east. Spanish settlers arrived from the 1570s to mine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and raise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> founded missions from 1596 at Santiago Papasquiaro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a> de Tepehuanes and later El Zape. Epidemics in 1594, 1601-1602, 1606-1607, 1610 and 1616-1617 may have reduced the Tepehuán and their neighbours from more than 100,000 people to fewer than 20,000.</p><p>In 1616 a baptised Tepehuán named Quautlatas began preaching through the mountains under the sign of a broken cross. Calling himself a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, he taught that the old gods were angry, that all Spaniards had to be killed or driven out, and that those killed by the Spanish would rise again after seven days. Many Tepehuán linked baptism with the disease and death that followed it; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> blamed the devil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 16 November 1616 the Tepehuán attacked a wagon train outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a> de Tepehuanes, and coordinated attacks under six war chiefs, the best known Francisco Gogoxito, followed across the region. About 100 people were killed in the church at El Zape and as many at Santiago Papasquiaro, and only one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> in Tepehuán territory survived the first attacks. Governor Gaspar de Alvear had 75 local Indigenous leaders executed and in December rescued 400 Spaniards and allies. The Spanish abandoned their policy of peace by purchase for a war of fire and blood, and killed Gogoxito, the last war chief, in March 1618.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>More than 200 Spaniards, 10 missionaries, an unknown number of their allies and perhaps 4,000 Tepehuán died, many of hunger and disease, and property worth as much as a million pesos was destroyed. The missionaries returned in 1618, though raids went on, and the revolt was declared over in 1620. The region took half a century to recover, and missions were afterwards better protected by soldiers. Three Tepehuán groups survive, in Chihuahua and southern Durango.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tepehuán Revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Missions of the Jesuits and Franciscans pushed into the Sierra Madre, provoking the <b>Tepehuán revolt</b> of 1616 and Tarahumara uprisings.</blockquote><a href="/state/nueva-vizcaya">Nueva Vizcaya</a></li></ul></section>
